WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.852
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:38.643
and I'm musician in residence at Yale

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.119
University's Fortunoff Video Archive

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.770
for Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.109
This video series is designed as a

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.239
companion to our album, Where Is

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.759
Our Homeland, which is the first volume

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.426
in the Archive's ongoing effort to

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.660
present songs and poems

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.623
performed by survivors in testimonies

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.625
given to the Fortunoff Archive over

00:00:58.310 --> 00:00:59.710
the last 40 years.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.969
You can learn more about the project on

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.170
the Archive's website,

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.860
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.089
Where Is Our Homeland?

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:09.349
Songs from Testimonies.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.529
Volume One was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.539
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble,

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:16.269
featuring renowned singer Sasha

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.580
Lurje, Craig Judelman on the violin,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.339
Joshua Camp on the accordion, keyboards

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:23.920
and guitar, Dmitry Ishenko

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:27.020
on the bass, and myself on woodwinds.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.569
The songs in this collection differ

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.138
vastly in terms of style, social

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.685
and historical background, language

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.629
and many other aspects.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.310
But collectively they create a

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.516
multidimensional picture of Jewish

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.820
life before and during World War II.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.559
In this episode, we will be

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.540
continuing talking about

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.179
and listening to the songs sung

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.550
by Liubov' K.,

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.759
a native of the Ukrainian town

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:56.770
of Zvenigorodka,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.509
a former inmate of the slave labor

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.990
camp in the same area.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.204
This musical project began

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.979
with Liubov's interview,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.160
with Liubov's testimony

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.340
and this song, Proshchay-

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.400
Farewell is written in Russian.

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.877
It was remembered by Liubov' as a

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.609
poem that people

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:23.933
who were-- who were leaving

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.690
their homes, abandoning

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.519
their dear

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.549
places that they loved

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.889
all their lives, they were passing

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.989
this poem to each other

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.440
in this way, sharing that sorrow

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.739
and sustaining and supporting

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.740
each other.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.590
I wrote music to this

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:50.190
poem in the genre of Salon

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.779
Romance, of the Bard song

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.346
so popular in the Soviet

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.600
Union, and

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.009
it came from the even earlier

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.140
era, from the 19th century

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:04.449
sentimentalism,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.109
sentimentalists song that was

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:10.509
very popular in the former

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.969
Russian empire, an urban genre

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.209
that was also shared by people

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.652
and was used to convey

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.829
earnest feelings.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.190
So Proshchay, Farewell.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.460
Farewell, our native city.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.700
Farewell, family so dear.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.820
Farewell, precious mothers.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.540
[wait…] (trying to remember)

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.980
Farewell, to all our friends.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.260
Blindly, we walk down the road,

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.900
Not knowing what lies ahead.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.180
The ominous thought is growing,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.340
That awaiting us is death.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.900
From the distant mountains,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.420
Nemorozh’s houses peer out.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.620
Heavily the heart begins to pound

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.700
And tears begin to fall.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.660
What a home we were given,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.300
A pig stall without a window or a door.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.620
A heavy wind blew right through us,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.820
And chilled us down to the bones.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.380
At first we were not used to it,

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.580
Such savagery, brutality.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.340
With sweat we toiled to till the earth

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.820
And watered it with tears.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:52.180
Farewell, our native city.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.300
Farewell, family so dear.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.780
Farewell, precious mothers.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.940
Farewell, to all our friends.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:14.020
Blindly, we walk down the road,

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.980
Not knowing what lies ahead.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.620
The ominous thought is growing,

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.580
That awaiting us is death.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:41.220
From the distant mountains,

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:46.540
Nemorozh’s houses peer out.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:52.900
Heavily the heart begins to pound

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.580
And tears begin to fall.

