What a home we were given,

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:30.100
A pig stall without a window or a door.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.980
A heavy wind blew right through us,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.620
And chilled us down to the bones.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:46.380
At first we were not used to it,

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:51.100
Such savagery, brutality.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.740
With sweat we toiled to till the earth

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:02.060
And watered it with tears.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:06.780
With sweat we toiled to till the earth

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:11.940
And watered it with tears.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:34.260
And watered it with tears.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.829
After launching Operation Barbarossa

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.589
on June 22, 1941,

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:54.815
the German army reached the central

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.300
Ukrainian town of Zvenigorodka

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.300
on July 29th.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:02.179
In 1939, the Jewish population

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:04.969
of the city had hovered just below 2,000,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.064
with Jews representing approximately

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.179
14% of the total population.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.700
Liubov' K. recalls that the first

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:15.979
few days of the German occupation

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.189
were relatively calm.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.299
However, the Germans soon began going

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.489
house to house to pillage and

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:24.700
to terrorize the local inhabitants.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.940
In September of that year, the Germans

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.580
forced all Jews in

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.420
Zvenigorodka to move into a ghetto which

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:34.939
occupied only two streets and one

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:35.940
alley.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.959
Conditions in the ghetto were so crowded

00:08:39.229 --> 00:08:40.879
that there were two to three families

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.319
living in each house.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.019
Although the Zvenigorodka ghetto was not

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.089
walled off, it was closely

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.489
guarded by Germans and local

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.940
auxiliary police known as the politsai.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:54.884
In the ghetto, young, able-bodied

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:56.859
Jews like Liubov' were assigned

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.869
to menial and humiliating tasks

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:01.729
such as cleaning latrines.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:05.060
As they worked, non-Jewish schoolchildren

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:07.060
taunted them by singing antisemitic

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.060
ditties and mocking their misfortunes.

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:13.630
On May 2, 1942,

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.269
the Jews of nearby Ol'shana were

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.219
brought to the Zvenigorodka ghetto.

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:21.279
Shortly thereafter, on May 5th,

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:23.659
the Ukrainian politsai and German

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.940
gendarmerie rounded up approximately

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.140
300 able-bodied Jews

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.549
from the ghetto and transferred them

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:32.420
to an improvised labor camp in

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.375
the village of Nemorozh, which lies

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:36.211
eight kilometers northwest of

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:37.800
Zvenigorodka.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.240
In her interview, Liubov' recalls,

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:43.879
"A German came to us

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.742
and said we should take two changes of

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:48.589
clothing, underwear, outerwear

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.580
and go to the Jewish Council."

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.120
There they would await their transfer to

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:55.580
an uncertain future.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:59.239
In late September, early October

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.759
of the previous year, a detachment

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.129
of Einsatzkommando 5 had rounded

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.200
up one hundred Jewish men from the

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.176
Zvenigorodka ghetto on the pretext

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.089
of taking them for labor somewhere else.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.600
Instead, they were shot in Laptev

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:15.060
meadow.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.220
This was the first mass shooting of

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.860
Zvenigorodka Jews.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.649
So in May 1942, when

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.020
Liubov' and approximately three hundred

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:26.420
other Jews were rounded up to be taken

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:29.119
for labor, they were understandably

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:30.510
prepared for the worst.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:34.330
It is this profound sense of anguishing

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.469
uncertainty that inspired the

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:38.180
words of their song Proshchay,

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.781
which Liubov' recites in her testimony.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.510
"Farewell our native city.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:46.999
Farewell, family so dear.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.136
Farewell, precious mothers.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:51.499
Farewell to all our friends.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.169
Blindly, we walk down the road,

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.320
not knowing what lies ahead.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:58.999
The ominous thought is growing that

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.550
awaiting us is death."

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:04.999
As it turned out, the Germans had

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.715
other plans for this group of three

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:09.650
hundred Zvenigorodka and Ol'shana Jews.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:12.183
Liubov' and her fellow prisoners were

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.539
taken to Nemorozh, where they were forced

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:16.700
to perform backbreaking labor on

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:18.869
the transit highway number IV,

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.539
which stretched from L'viv in the west

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.090
to Taganrog in Russia

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.789
in the east, with tributary roads

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:28.230
running north to south.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.739
Liubov' and the other Jewish prisoners in

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:33.700
the Nemorozh camp worked on one

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:35.740
such tributary road, which

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:37.329
led north to Kiev.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.313
The laborers were given only the most

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:42.849
rudimentary of tools, shovels

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:45.189
and picks, with which they were asked

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:47.340
to build, maintain and repair

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:48.580
the highway.

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:50.933
And while the labor they provided was

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.459
valuable, the German overseers

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.709
and local auxiliary police who guarded

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.470
them saw these prisoners as expendable.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.540
To say the conditions in the

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.900
Nemorozh camp were rustic would be a

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.900
profound understatement.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.430
The prisoners lived in an unheated barn

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.140
and as Liubov' recalls,

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:13.500
"we slept directly on the ground.

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:15.500
There was nothing to cover ourselves

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:18.060
with. So we returned from work

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.180
in our clothes and ate the thin

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:21.540
gruel that they gave us.

00:12:22.410 --> 00:12:24.076
They cooked it for us twice a day,

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:26.279
morning and evening, no salt,

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:28.500
a quarter of a bucket of millet

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.739
to 17 buckets of water."

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.617
The prisoner's daily struggle for

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.289
survival under these conditions inspired

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.620
the words of their song, "such

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.359
a home we were given, a pig stall

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:44.070
without a window or a door.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.979
A heavy wind blew right through us.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.080
And chilled us down to the bone.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:51.910
At first we were not used to it,

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.169
such savagery, brutality.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:56.700
With sweat, we toil to till the

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:59.414
Earth and watered it with tears."

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:04.080
In the evenings after work ended,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.479
Liubov' and her fellow prisoners passed

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.340
the time by singing and composing songs.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.260
This process of writing

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:14.859
songs and sharing them boosted

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:16.869
their morale immeasurably as they endured

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.540
daily hardships and losses,

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.060
giving voice to their feelings of hope

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.020
and despair.

